  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 403 AM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nModels push a TUTT feature over the state further eastward, while a \nsfc frontal boundary moves across the SE CONUS. Meanwhile, the W Atl \nridge axis will linger just north of the state through the rest of \nthe week, keeping a generally E/SE flow in place across SoFlo. \n\nMoisture advection will persist during the long term and keeping \ntropical moisture in place, with model PWATs remaining above \nseasonal normals around or above 2\" through the work week. This will \ncontinue to support scattered to numerous showers and storms each \nafternoon, with convection initiating along the East coast sea \nbreeze boundary, then pushing inland during the late afternoon \nhours. Hazards will include gusty winds, heavy rainfall and frequent \nlightning.\n\nThrough mid week, models show the aforementioned trough and front \nprogressing eastward, with the two flanking ridges over the CONUS \nand western Atlantic starting to merge, extending across the entire \nregion. Afternoon showers and thunderstorms will remain likely each \nafternoon, with coverage maximized across the interior and southwest \nFL thanks to the continuing easterly flow. The wet pattern should \nextend through the end of the work week with abundant moisture \nremaining in place. \n\nTemperatures through the period will remain seasonably hot, with \nhighs in the low to mid 90s each afternoon and overnight lows in the \nlow 80s across the coastlines, and mid 70s over the interior.",
